{"timestamp":"2026-07-11T20:29:32.348Z","ordinal":157,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"019f52de-5e71-74d3-bd28-d51c57c0b7e3","turn_id":"019f52de-5ebd-7b31-a4ae-90fc9573af35","item":{"type":"WebSearch","id":"exec-4ef67068-667b-4475-abfb-9d8ee1e3ab27","query":"site:sidefx.com/docs/houdini/character/apex Python ...","action":{"type":"search","queries":["site:sidefx.com/docs/houdini/character/apex Python","site:sidefx.com/docs/houdini \"APEX\" \"Python\"","site:sidefx.com/docs/houdini \"APEX Scene Animate\" procedural asset","site:sidefx.com/docs/houdini \"APEX\" \"Houdini Engine\" Unreal","site:sidefx.com/docs/houdini/character/kinefx live link Unreal control rigs"]}},"completed_at_ms":1783801772348}}
{"timestamp":"2026-07-11T20:29:32.354Z","ordinal":158,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call_output","call_id":"call_ZpT9pNVbhaseJy2aPjGVuZMR","output":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Script completed\nWall time 4.3 seconds\nOutput:\n"},{"type":"input_text","text":"\"Houdini-LiveLink plug-in installation (https://www.sidefx.com/docs/houdini/unreal/livelink.html)\\nciteturn21search0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 days ago; The default location is \\\\Side Effects Software\\\\Houdini X.Y.Z\\\\engine\\\\unreal\\\\U.E\\\\HoudiniLiveLink. ... Select the checkbox for Live Link and Houdini KineFX LiveLink.\\n\\n# Houdini-LiveLink plug-in installation\\n\\nInstall and setup for Houdini LiveLink\\n\\nOn this page  | \\n\\n  * Installation\\n    * Houdini Launcher\\n    * Old Installer\\n    * Github Repository\\n  * Enable LiveLink in Unreal\\n  * Compatibility\\n  * Using LiveLink\\n\\nThe Houdini Live Link plugin adds a new Live Link source that lets you controls rigs in Unreal directly from Houdini.\\n\\n## Installation ¶\\n\\nThere are three ways to download the Houdini Live Link Plugin:\\n\\n  * Houdini Launcher\\n\\n  * Old Installer\\n\\n  * Github Repository\\n\\n### Houdini Launcher ¶\\n\\n  1. Install the launcher if not already installed.\\n\\n  2. Open the launcher.\\n\\n  3. Click Houdini ▸ Install.\\n\\n  4. In the Choose a Version to Install, select either Production Build or Daily Build depending on your desired build version. If you need to select a specific version then select `Choose Another Version` and install the version desired.\\n\\n  5. In the Preferences pop-up window, turn on Unreal Plug-in and select install.\\n\\n  6. Locate the Houdini Engine directory. The default location is \\\\Side Effects Software\\\\Houdini Engine\\\\X.Y.Z.\\\\U.E\\\\HoudiniLiveLink.\\n\\n     * X.Y.Z. means your Houdini version.\\n\\n     * U.E. means your Unreal Engine version.\\n\\n  7. Copy the HoudiniLiveLink folder to the Plugins\\\\Animations folder of your Unreal directory. You can either copy it to Unreal Engine’s plugins directory or your Unreal project’s Plugin directory.\\n\\n     * Unreal’s directory location is \\\\Epic Games\\\\UE5.X\\\\Engine\\\\Plugins\\\\Animations and the project directory is \\\\Unreal Project\\\\Plugins\\\\Animations.\\n\\nNote\\n\\nFor Unreal Engine 5.X, you must use Unreal’s project directory.\\n\\n### Old Installer ¶\\n\\n  1. On SideFX Download select a download version. You can select the old installer, production builds, or daily builds.\\n\\n  2. In the Houdini installation setup, locate the Houdini Engine section. Select the checkbox for Houdini Engine for Unreal.\\n\\n  3. Locate the Houdini Engine directory. The default location is \\\\Side Effects Software\\\\Houdini X.Y.Z\\\\engine\\\\unreal\\\\U.E\\\\HoudiniLiveLink.\\n\\n     * X.Y.Z. means your Houdini version.\\n\\n     * U.E. means your Unreal Engine version.\\n\\n  4. Copy the HoudiniLiveLink folder to the Plugins\\\\Animations folder of your Unreal directory. You can either copy it to Unreal Engine’s plugins directory or your Unreal project’s Plugin directory.\\n\\n     * Unreal’s directory location is \\\\Epic Games\\\\UE5.X\\\\Engine\\\\Plugins\\\\Animations and the project directory is \\\\Unreal Project\\\\Plugins\\\\Animations.\\n\\nNote\\n\\nFor Unreal Engine 5.X, you must use Unreal’s project directory.\\n\\n### Github Repository ¶\\n\\nYou can also download the plugin’s binaries from its Git repository in the Releases section.\\n\\nAfter downloading the plugin’s release binaries, extract the archive and copy the HoudiniLiveLink folder to the Engine/Plugins/Animations folder in Unreal.\\n\\n## Enable LiveLink in Unreal ¶\\n\\nTo turn on the Live Link plugin in Unreal:\\n\\n  1. In Unreal’s main toolbar, select Edit, then Plugins.\\n\\n  2. Locate the Animation tab.\\n\\n  3. Select the checkbox for Live Link and Houdini KineFX LiveLink.\\n\\n  4. Restart Unreal.\\n\\nImage\\n\\n## Compatibility ¶\\n\\nBinaries and Source Code are available for Unreal 4.26.2, 4.27, and 5.0. The UE4_LiveLink HDA requires Houdini 19.5 or above since it uses KineFX.\\n\\n## Using LiveLink ¶\\n\\n  1. After installing the plugin, a new Houdini Live Link source will be available.\\n\\n     * The LiveLink plugin should be used with the `ue4_livelink` HDA, available in the plugin’s content directory.\\n\\n  2. After importing the HDA in Houdini, start the LiveLink server directly on the live link node.\\n\\n  3. In Unreal, add a new Live Link source in Windows ▸ Live Link ▸ Add. The Houdini Live Link source will now be available and can be used as an animation controller.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nAPEX Scene Animate (https://www.sidefx.com/docs/houdini/nodes/sop/apex--sceneanimate.html)\\nciteturn21search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; This allows for procedural chaining of animation using multiple APEX Scene Animate SOPs.\\n\\n# APEX Scene Animate geometry node\\n\\nEdits the animation on an APEX scene.\\n\\nOn this page  | \\n\\n  * Parameters\\n  * Inputs\\n  * Outputs\\nSince  | 20.0\\n\\nThis node allows you to edit the animation on an APEX scene using the animate state, the viewer state on this node. See the animate state page for information on the available tools and functionality for animating, and the animation workflow for examples of how to use this node.\\n\\n## Parameters ¶\\n\\nInherit Animation Layers from Input\\n\\nWhen turned on, inherits animation layers that are added upstream from this node. The inherited layers are read-only within this node, but upstream changes to the layers are propagated down and merged into this node. This allows for procedural chaining of animation using multiple APEX Scene Animate SOPs. Inherited animation layers are not written out to the cache.\\n\\nWhen turned off, copies the upstream layers to this node. These layers are not inherited, so they can be edited, and any upstream changes are not reflected in this node.\\n\\nEdit Animation\\n\\nOpens a window that allows you to stash, delete, and rename the animation clips in the current scene.\\n\\nReset Animation\\n\\nRemoves the animation that was added in the animate state. If no animation was previously added to the scene upstream from this node, the animation folder is deleted from the scene packed folder structure, which can be viewed in the rig tree view.\\n\\nReset Custom\\n\\nRemoves specific scene elements that were added in the animate state.\\n\\nReset All\\n\\nRemoves all changes made in the animate state, including the animation and constraints that were added. Deletes the animation and constraints folders from the scene packed folder structure.\\n\\n## Inputs ¶\\n\\nScene\\n\\nThe animation scene to add animation to.\\n\\n## Outputs ¶\\n\\nScene\\n\\nThe animation scene with the added animation.\\n\\nSee also  | \\n\\n# Geometry nodes--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nKineFX (https://www.sidefx.com/docs/houdini/character/kinefx/index.html)\\nciteturn21search2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 days ago; You can create your own KineFX characters from scratch, or use special KineFX SOPs and regular Houdini SOPs to edit imported characters and animation. ... The KineFX framework is built on the principles of procedural rigging. ... This animation scene is then passed to an APEX Scene Animate SOP, where animation can be performed in the node’s animate state.\\n\\n# KineFX\\n\\nProcedural rigging and animation with SOP-based characters and rigs.\\n\\nImage\\n\\n#### What is KineFX?\\n\\nKineFX is a rigging and animation framework and toolset that allows you to create and edit characters all at the geometry level. You can create your own KineFX characters from scratch, or use special KineFX SOPs and regular Houdini SOPs to edit imported characters and animation.\\n\\n  * For definitions of KineFX terminology, see the Glossary.\\n\\n#### Procedural rigging\\n\\nThe KineFX framework is built on the principles of procedural rigging. Procedural rigging allows you to iterate on your rigs quickly, experiment non-destructively, and explore set-ups without fear of breaking anything like you would a static graph system. With KineFX, you can reconfigure a rig while animating, delete entire parts of an animated hierarchy and not lose its skin or animation, or unparent all your points and retain their positions (world space transforms).\\n\\nAPEX (All-Purpose EXecution) is the graph evaluation framework that is used for building and evaluating character rigs. APEX graphs are geometry that are used to represent a character’s rig logic. Users can procedurally build and assemble rig logic pieces, and choose to evaluate the rig logic at a later point in the network. This idea of delayed evaluation allows the rig logic and rig evaluation to be decoupled, resulting in significant performance improvements.\\n\\nKineFX is the SOP-level rigging and animation framework, and APEX is the graph evaluation engine that runs underneath it. KineFX makes use of APEX, with some of the KineFX infrastructure now using APEX graph evaluation.\\n\\nImage\\n\\n#### Animation and the packed character folder structure\\n\\nImage Packed character folder structure in the rig tree view\\n\\nTo support the APEX animation and rigging workflows, the packed character folder structure is a way to define, bundle, and organize the data that is needed to create and animate one or more characters in the viewport. The packed character folder structure consists of geometry data that is organized similar to a directory structure on disk, with the data added as nested packed primitives.\\n\\n  * Character folder structure\\n\\n## Rigging with APEX graphs ¶\\n\\n  * APEX graph basics\\n\\n  * APEX Script basics for graph building\\n\\n  * Graphs for numerical operations\\n\\n  * Graphs for viewport animation\\n\\n  * Graphs for deforming geometry\\n\\n  * Graph metadata\\n\\n  * APEX path pattern\\n\\n  * APEX graph nodes\\n\\nAPEX nodes provide operations for building up the functionality of APEX graphs, which are used in KineFX to create character rigs and perform other geometry manipulation.\\n\\n  * APEX updates for H21\\n\\n## Building rig graphs with APEX Script ¶\\n\\n  * Working with graphs\\n\\n  * Functions in APEX Script\\n\\n  * Creating graph interfaces\\n\\n  * Language reference\\n\\n## Rigging with rig components ¶\\n\\n  * Rigging with the Autorig Builder\\n\\n  * Rigging a simple geometry\\n\\n  * Rigging a character\\n\\n  * Working with blend shapes\\n\\n  * Creating custom rig functionality\\n\\n  * List of pre-built rig components\\n\\n## Animating in the viewport ¶\\n\\n  * Animation workflow\\n\\n  * Working in the animate state\\n\\n  * Posing\\n\\n  * Motion paths\\n\\n  * Constraints\\n\\n  * Dynamic motion\\n\\n  * Ragdoll\\n\\n  * Full body IK\\n\\n  * Animate state menus and HUDs\\n\\n  * Animate state hotkeys\\n\\n  * Control selection sets\\n\\nA tree view of controls, selections, and selection sets for the animate state. Allows management of control selection and visibility.\\n\\n  * Motion mixer\\n\\n  * Animation catalog\\n\\n  * Animation layers\\n\\n  * Animation toolbar\\n\\n  * Animation bookmarks\\n\\n## SOP-based animation ¶\\n\\n  * Physics-based motion\\n\\n  * Ragdoll simulations\\n\\n## Deformation ¶\\n\\n  * Blend shapes\\n\\n## Animation retargeting ¶\\n\\n  * Importing animation and character data\\n\\n  * Transferring animation\\n\\n  * Refining the retarget results\\n\\n  * Posing characters and layering animation\\n\\n  * Working with motion capture\\n\\n  * Adding secondary motion effects\\n\\n  * MotionClips\\n\\n  * Exporting animation and character data\\n\\n## Pre-H20 ¶\\n\\n\\n# Character\\n\\n## KineFX characters ¶\\n\\nGeometry-level procedural rigging and animation with SOP-based rigs.\\n\\n  * Procedural rigging\\n\\n  * Animation\\n\\n  * Animation retargeting\\n\\n## Object-level characters ¶\\n\\n(Deprecated) Object-level rigging and animation with bone-based rigs.\\n\\n  * Rigging\\n\\n  * Animation\\n\\n## Panes ¶\\n\\n  * Rig tree view\\n\\n  * Character picker\\n\\n  * Pose library\\n\\n  * Object autorigs (Deprecated)\\n\\n  * Object pose-space deformation (Deprecated)--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nKineFX character toolset (https://www.sidefx.com/docs/houdini/character/kinefx/overview.html)\\nciteturn21search3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; Image Pre-Houdini 20 KineFX rigging ... The “Building graphs with APEX Script” section of the KineFX documentation provides additional information and examples.\\n## Character elements ¶\\n\\n### Creating and preparing a skeleton for rigging ¶\\n\\nA character’s skeleton is represented at the geometry level by a collection of points connected by polygon lines. KineFX’s joint-based system allows a character’s joint hierarchy to be created and edited in various ways using SOP nodes. See working with skeletons for more information.\\n\\nImage Character skin (left), KineFX skeleton (right)\\n\\nTo prepare a skeleton for rigging, extra information can be added to a character’s base skeleton to create a reference skeleton, called a guide skeleton. The information on the guide skeleton is then picked up by rig components downstream to build up the rig functionality of the character. Some of the information that could be added are:\\n\\n  * Extra joints that are used as a reference for creating controls on the rig.\\n\\n  * Tags (labels) that identify groups of joints.\\n\\n  * Properties that set information on the guide skeleton’s joints.\\n\\nSee preparing skeletons for rigging for more information on how to create a guide skeleton.\\n\\nImage Skeleton tags\\n\\n### Creating capture weights on geometry ¶\\n\\nOnce we have a character’s skeleton and skin, we can use skin capture to create weights on the geometry for skin deformation. When the joints move, the geometry is deformed according to the capture weights and position of the joints. See skin capture for the different SOPs that are available for creating and modifying capture weights.\\n\\nImage Deformed geometry using capture weights\\n\\n### Procedural rigging ¶\\n\\nRigging in KineFX is driven by APEX (All-Purpose EXecution), a graph evaluation framework that builds and runs character rigs. In APEX, character rigs are represented using APEX graphs.\\n\\n#### A bit of background ¶\\n\\nPre-Houdini 20, a character’s rig logic lived in rig behavior nodes (like the Rig Pose SOP) and was also evaluated on those nodes. Once we came to the point in the network that determined how a rig behaved, this functionality had to be evaluated at that exact place in the network. \\nImage Rigging with APEX\\n\\n#### Building rigs in APEX ¶\\n\\nThere are several ways to build your character rig in APEX, and these different methods can be combined to construct your final rig:\\n\\nDrop down and connect graph nodes in the APEX UI  | The APEX network view is the UI where you can put down APEX graph nodes and manually connect them to build a graph. For more information on how to do this, start with the APEX graph basics page, then go through the graph examples for numerical operations, viewport animation, and geometry deformation.\\nUse the APEX Script code interface  | APEX Script is a code interface that uses a Python-like syntax to generate APEX graphs. APEX Script snippets can be written in the APEX Script SOP and APEX Autorig Component SOP to add custom functionality to a rig. For an introduction to APEX Script, see the APEX Script basics page. The “Building graphs with APEX Script” section of the KineFX documentation provides additional information and examples.\\nUse the Autorig Builder  | Build a complete rig in the Autorig Builder by interactively applying and configuring rig components in the viewport.\\nUse pre-built rig components  | Stay in the SOP network and use pre-built rig components to automatically build parts of the rig as APEX graphs under the hood. These rig components are available from the APEX Autorig Component SOP.\\n\\n#### Rigging documentation ¶\\n\\nBelow is a snapshot of the rigging documentation:\\n\\n##### Rigging with APEX graphs ¶\\n\\n  * The APEX graph basics page describes the elements that make up an APEX graph, and how to manually create graphs in the APEX network view.\\n\\n  * The APEX Script basics page gives an introduction to APEX Script with some simple examples.\\n\\n  * Graphs for numerical operations gives step-by-step instructions on how to create and execute APEX graphs for numerical operations like adding two values, fetching multiple results, and retrieving information from geometry.\\n\\n  * Create your own functions in APEX Script or use the built-in APEX functions within your code snippet. APEX Script also provides convenient name mappings for some of the built-in APEX functions.\\n\\n  * Use APEX Script to create and customize interfaces for your graphs.\\n\\n  * The APEX Script language reference describes the syntax and language elements of APEX Script, including the provided types, operations, and statements.\\n\\n##### Rigging with rig components ¶\\n\\n  * Quickly and interactively build up a complete rig for a character using the Autorig Builder.\\n\\n  * Learn how to use Houdini’s pre-built rig components to rig a 3-joint tube geometry.\\n\\n  * Use pre-built rig components to build up a rig for a character.\\n\\n  * Walk through how to set up blend shapes and create custom sliders for the blend shape rig component.\\n\\n  * Learn different ways to create custom rig functionality.\\n\\n  * See the list of available pre-built rig components here.\\n\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nGetting animation and character data out of KineFX (https://www.sidefx.com/docs/houdini/character/kinefx/exportcharacters.html)\\nciteturn21search4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 days ago; You can use the Houdini KineFX LiveLink plug-in for Unreal Engine to control rigs in Unreal Editor from KineFX in Houdini. ... On the most recent Houdini Live Link release’s web page, scroll down to the Assets section and then click the HoudiniLiveLink `.zip` that matches the version of Unreal Editor you are running. ... In Houdini, open the scene that contains the KineFX animated skeleton or character that you want to drive the skeleton or character in Unreal Editor.\\n\\n# Getting animation and character data out of KineFX\\n\\nOn this page  | \\n\\n  * Pack SOP characters or save to disk\\n  * Bring SOP character data into Crowds\\n  * Export SOP character data for use outside of Houdini\\n  * Bring SOP character data to the object-level\\n  * Bring SOP character data into Solaris\\n  * Control rigs in Unreal Editor with KineFX\\n    * How to\\n  * Turn a SOP character into an HDA\\n    * How to\\n\\nWhen you have finished retargeting your SOP animation skeletons and characters, you may need to use their data elsewhere in KineFX or SOPs, hand them off to a different artist, bring them to a different context in Houdini, or export them for use in another DCC.\\n\\nTo help you with this process, there are several ways to bundle, export, or import from your SOP characters, animation skeletons, or MotionClips.\\n\\n## Pack SOP characters or save to disk ¶\\n\\n  * If you want to use your SOP character elsewhere in your network, in a different network, or in a different Geometry node, you can bundle it as a packed geometry primitive with the Image Character Pack SOP node.\\n\\n  * Use the Animated Pose parameter to specify the path to the SOP node that contains the animated skeleton geometry for your character. This would be the third input to the Joint Deform node in SOPs.\\n\\n  * (Optional) Use the Rest Geometry parameter to specify the path to the SOP node that contains the rest skin geometry for your character. This would be the first input to the Joint Deform node or the Character Blend Shapes node (if your character has blend shapes) in SOPs. If you leave this field empty, then only the skeleton and animation data for your character will be imported.\\n\\n  * (Optional) Use the Capture Pose parameter to specify the path to the SOP node that contains the capture pose you want to use for the Rest Geometry. This would be the second input to the Joint Deform node in SOPs.\\n\\n## Control rigs in Unreal Editor with KineFX ¶\\n\\nYou can use the Houdini KineFX LiveLink plug-in for Unreal Engine to control rigs in Unreal Editor from KineFX in Houdini.\\n\\nHoudini source requirements  | \\n\\n  * Skeleton with a rest pose (for example, from the skeleton or a Image Rig Stash Pose SOP).\\n  * Animation (for example, Image FBX Anim Import SOP, Image FBX Character Import SOP, Image Mocap Import SOP, Image Mocap Stream SOP, Image Rig Pose SOP, animation output from a MotionClip or animation retargeting network, and so on).\\nUnreal Editor target requirements  | \\n\\n  * Skeleton that matches the Houdini source skeleton (number of joints, joint names, and so on).\\n\\n### How to ¶\\n\\n  1. Open a web browser window and navigate to the Houdini LiveLink web page on github.\\n\\n  2. In the right-hand column, click the most recent release listed under Releases.\\n\\nImage\\n\\n  3. On the most recent Houdini Live Link release’s web page, scroll down to the Assets section and then click the HoudiniLiveLink `.zip` that matches the version of Unreal Editor you are running.\\n\\nImage\\n\\nThe `.zip` archive file automatically downloads to your local `\\\\Downloads` folder.\\n\\n  4. Extract the HoudiniLiveLink.zip archive you downloaded, dive into the unzipped folder, and then do the following:\\n\\n     * Copy the HoudiniLiveLink folder (for example, `HoudiniLiveLink-UE-4.26`) to the `\\\\Engine\\\\Plugins\\\\Animation` folder in your Unreal Editor install director.\\n\\n     * Navigate to the `\\\\Content` folder and then save the `ue4_livelink.hda` file to where you keep your HDAs for import into your scenes.\\n\\n  5. In Houdini, open the scene that contains the KineFX animated skeleton or character that you want to drive the skeleton or character in Unreal Editor.\\n\\n  6. Select File ▸ Import ▸ Houdini Digital Asset.\\n\\nImage\\n\\n  7. In the Install Digital Asset Library window, use the Digital Asset Library option to browse for and select the `ue4_livelink.hda` file you downloaded, and then click Install and Create.\\n\\nImage\\n\\nThe `ue4_livelink` HDA appears in the Network Editor.\\n\\n  8. In the Network Editor, connect the animated pose output of your animated skeleton or character to the input of the `ue4_livelink` HDA.\\n\\nImage\\n\\n  9. Select the `ue4_livelink` HDA and then open the Parameter Editor.\\n\\n  10. In the Parameter Editor, note the Port and IP Address for later in this procedure and then click Start Server.\\n\\nImage\\n\\nImage\\n\\n  11. Run Unreal Editor.\\n\\n  12. In Unreal Editor, select Edit ▸ Configuration ▸ Plugins from the main menu.\\n\\nImage\\n\\nThe Plugins configuration window appears.\\n\\n  13. In the Plugins configuration window, navigate to the Animation section, turn on (enable) the LiveLink and Houdini KineFX LiveLink plugins, and then click Restart Now to restart your Unreal Editor session.\\n\\nImage\\n\\n  14. In Unreal Editor, load the Unreal skeleton or rigged character that you want to drive with your KineFX animated skeleton or character.\\n\\n  15. In the main menu, select Window ▸ LiveLink.\\n\\nImage\\n\\nThe LiveLink tab appears.\\n\\nImage LiveLink tab in Unreal Editor\\n\\n  16. In the Live Link tab, click Source ▸ Houdini LiveLink.\\n\\n  17. In the Live Link information fly-out menu that appears, verify that the Port Number is a combination of the Port and IP Address on the `ue4_livelink` HDA. If it is not, replace it with the correct information.\\n\\nImage Example: LiveLink source Port Number in Unreal Editor\\n\\nImage Example: ue4_livelink HDA Port and IP Address in Houdini\\n\\n  18. Click Add Source.\\n\\nImage\\n\\nIf the subject appears below with a green dot next to it, then the connection between Houdini and Unreal was successfully established.\\n\\nNote\\n\\nThe green dot may take a few moments to appear.\\n\\nImage\\n\\n  19. In the Preview Scene Settings tab, expand the Animation section and do the following:\\n\\n     * Select Preview Controller ▸ Live Link Preview Controller.\\n\\n     * Select Live Link Subject ▸ Houdini Subject.\\n\\nImage Preview Scene Settings tab in Unreal Editor\\n\\nThe source animated skeleton or character in Houdini is now connected to the target Unreal skeleton or rigged character.\\n\\n  20. In Houdini, playback your animation.\\n\\nThe target skeleton or rigged character in Unreal is now driven by the source animated skeleton or animated character from Houdini.\\n\\nExample: Houdini animated character driving the Erik’s animation in Unreal Editor. Character model courtesy of Christophe Desse.\\n\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nBringing animation and character data into KineFX (https://www.sidefx.com/docs/houdini/character/kinefx/importcharacters.html)\\nciteturn21search5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 days ago;   * Stream live motion capture data directly from Faceware Studio, Live Link Face, Optitrack Motive, Perception Neuron, Rokoko Studio, Vicon Shogun, or Xsens MVN with a Mocap Stream SOP. ... If your imported animation skeleton or character is dramatically larger or smaller than it should be, your animation skeleton or character may come from a source that uses different world units than Houdini (for example, meters vs. centimeters).To fix this issue, turn on the Convert Units parameter on the KineFX import node you used to bring in your character or motion data. ... If you import a character rig and the control objects don’t come through or the links between the control geometry and joints are broken, you need to reattach the control geometry to your character or attach new controls.\\n\\n# Bringing animation and character data into KineFX\\n\\nOn this page  | \\n\\n  * Unpack SOP characters or import from disk\\n  * Bring in motion capture data\\n  * Bring in agents from Crowds\\n  * Bring in USD data from Solaris or other sources\\n  * Import FBX data\\n  * Import glTF data\\n  * Bring in object-level character data or HDAs\\n  * Bring in character data from other DCCs\\n  * Troubleshooting your characters after importing into KineFX\\n    * Character’s global scale is off\\n    * Skeleton is broken into separate joint chains\\n    * Control objects are missing or garbled\\n\\nTo perform an animation retargeting operation, you first need to bring in the source animation skeleton or character you want to retarget from, and the character you want to retarget to. This page describes several ways to import animation skeletons, captures (skins), and characters into KineFX.\\n\\n## Unpack SOP characters or import from disk ¶\\n\\n  * If you have a stashed or bundled animation skeleton or character (stored in a Stash SOP or Object Merge SOP), you can unpack the character data with a Character Unpack SOP.\\n\\n  * If you want to unpack a SOP character’s data stream that is saved on disk and then have the option of using the sequence on disk or in a live stream, you can use a Character I/O SOP.\\n\\n  * If you want to import a SOP animation skeleton or character that is saved in a file on disk, use a File SOP.\\n\\n## Bring in motion capture data ¶\\n\\nYou can bring motion capture data into KineFX in the following ways:\\n\\n  * Import Acclaim, BioVision, or Motion Analysis motion capture animation files with a Mocap Import SOP or through Houdini’s File ▸ Import ▸ Motion Capture.\\n\\n  * Stream live motion capture data directly from Faceware Studio, Live Link Face, Optitrack Motive, Perception Neuron, Rokoko Studio, Vicon Shogun, or Xsens MVN with a Mocap Stream SOP.\\n\\n  * Stream live raw motion capture data (UDP or TCP) with a Mocap Stream SOP.\\n\\nFor more information on importing and streaming motion capture to KineFX, see working with motion capture.\\n\\n## Bring in agents from Crowds ¶\\n\\nYou can bring agents or agent clips into KineFX for use with SOP characters and MotionClips in the following ways:\\n\\n  * Generate a SOP animation skeleton from an agent with an Agent Animation Unpack SOP.\\n\\n  * Create a MotionClip or multiple MotionClips (packed MotionClip) from an agent with an Agent Animation Unpack SOP.\\n\\n  * Generate a SOP character from an agent with an Agent Character Unpack SOP.\\n\\nTip\\n\\nIf the animation skeleton generated by the Agent Animation Unpack SOP has a different joint hierarchy than the SOP character you want to retarget to, you can constrain the animation skeleton’s joints to the target character using a Rig Copy Transforms SOP.\\n\\n## Bring in USD data from Solaris or other sources ¶\\n\\nIf your imported animation skeleton or character is dramatically larger or smaller than it should be, your animation skeleton or character may come from a source that uses different world units than Houdini (for example, meters vs. centimeters). To fix this issue, turn on the Convert Units parameter on the KineFX import node you used to bring in your character or motion data.\\n\\nIf your source and target characters are dramatically different sizes, you can use the Rig Match Pose SOP’s bounding box matching to automatically resize and conform their skeletons.\\n\\n### Skeleton is broken into separate joint chains ¶\\n\\nIf you import an animation skeleton or character into KineFX and parts of the hierarchy are no longer attached, you need to reattach the broken joint chains using a Skeleton SOP or Parent Joints SOP.\\n\\nReconnect joints using the Skeleton SOP Reconnect joints using the Parent Joints SOP\\n\\n### Control objects are missing or garbled ¶\\n\\nIf you import a character rig and the control objects don’t come through or the links between the control geometry and joints are broken, you need to reattach the control geometry to your character or attach new controls. For more information, see setting up control geometry.\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nanimation | SideFX (https://www.sidefx.com/products/houdini/characters/animation/)\\nciteturn21search6 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; APEX Scene Add Animation ...  Houdini CORE     |       Houdini FX     |      Houdini Indie     |      Houdini Education     |      Houdini Apprentice\\n\\n.video-bg-control {position:absolute;} .video-bg-control button {cursor:pointer;background:transparent;} .video-bg-control.top-right { top:20px;right:20px; } .video-bg-control.top-left { top:20px;left:20px; } .video-bg-control.bottom-right { bottom:20px;right:20px; } .video-bg-control.bottom-left { bottom:20px;left:20px; } .video-bg-control svg { width:40px; height:40px; } .video-bg-control.hide, .video-audio-play.hide , .video-bg-play.hide { display:none } #EUaf6Tw_control * { stroke: rgba(255,255,255,0.93); } .is-light-text #EUaf6Tw_control * { stroke: rgba(255,255,255,0.93); } .is-dark-text #EUaf6Tw_control * { stroke: #000; }\\n\\n## Ragdolls\\n\\nTurning on Ragdolls in APEX Animate gives you access to simulated motion on your characters and props. This can help you manage complex motion and layer it into your characters.\\n\\n### Ragdoll Prop\\n\\nLayer ragdoll motion to explore options as you animate.\\n\\n### Ragdoll Boxing\\n\\nUse ragdoll dynamics on all of your character or on part of a character.\\n\\n### Ragdoll Pose\\n\\nRadgoll posing creates character interactions using dynamics.\\n\\n## Animation Nodes\\n\\nHere some of the nodes to look out for when working with Animation in KineFX. These nodes let you add and animate characters and props. The Scene Animate node is where the animation interface becomes available to artists.\\n\\nAPEX Scene Animate\\n\\nAPEX Scene Add Character\\n\\nAPEX Scene Invoke\\n\\nAPEX Scene Add Prop\\n\\nAPEX Scene Add Animation\\n\\nAPEX Control Extract\\n\\nAPEX Control Update Parms\\n\\nAPEX Animation from Skeleton\\n\\n#### \\n\\n#### \\n\\n#### LEARNING RESOURCES\\n\\nCheck out a growing collection of lessons focused on Animation in Houdini. \\n\\nAnimation Learning Path\\n\\nCharacter Animation\\n\\nRagdoll\\n\\nProject Violet\\n\\nProject Outback\\n\\n* * *\\n\\nANIMATION  FEATURES AVAILABLE IN:\\n\\n Houdini CORE     |       Houdini FX     |      Houdini Indie     |      Houdini Education     |      Houdini Apprentice--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nCharacter (https://www.sidefx.com/docs/houdini/character/index.html)\\nciteturn21search7 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 days ago; How to rig and animate characters in Houdini. ... ## KineFX characters ¶ ... ## Character FX ¶\\n\\n# Character\\n\\nHow to rig and animate characters in Houdini.\\n\\n## Subtopics ¶\\n\\n## KineFX characters ¶\\n\\nGeometry-level procedural rigging and animation with SOP-based rigs.\\n\\n  * Procedural rigging\\n\\n  * Animation\\n\\n  * Animation retargeting\\n\\n## Object-level characters ¶\\n\\n(Deprecated) Object-level rigging and animation with bone-based rigs.\\n\\n  * Rigging\\n\\n  * Animation\\n\\n## Panes ¶\\n\\n  * Rig tree view\\n\\n  * Character picker\\n\\n  * Pose library\\n\\n  * Object autorigs (Deprecated)\\n\\n  * Object pose-space deformation (Deprecated)\\n\\n## Character FX ¶\\n\\n  * Character\\n\\nHow to rig and animate characters in Houdini.\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nWorking in the animate state (https://www.sidefx.com/docs/houdini/character/kinefx/animatestate.html)\\nciteturn21search8 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; Houdini 21.0 Character KineFX ... The animate state is a viewer state that can display multiple characters in an animation scene and allow users to perform posing such as FK or IK for characters, interact with controls on characters, and add constraints between characters. ... To enter the animate state, select the APEX Scene Animate SOP in the network editor, turn on its display flag, and click Image Animate on the left toolbar.\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nPosing in the animate state (https://www.sidefx.com/docs/houdini/character/kinefx/animatestateposing.html)\\nciteturn21search9 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 4 days ago; Houdini 21.0 Character KineFX ... Using Live Preview without Outline Overlay does not work well with singular controls, as singular controls are itself mirrored, which results in the display of the rig switching between the original control position and the mirrored result every time the control is moved.\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nAnimate state menus and HUDs (https://www.sidefx.com/docs/houdini/character/kinefx/animatestatesettings.html)\\nciteturn21search10 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 weeks ago; Houdini 21.0 Character KineFX ... The benefit is that you have direct, independent control over the three rotation components, so you can use the handles to set up your animation without worrying about Houdini changing the other components.\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nCreating custom rig functionality (https://www.sidefx.com/docs/houdini/character/kinefx/customrigs.html)\\nciteturn21search11 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 days ago; The APEX Graph SOP can take in a character folder structure and automatically find the rig graph in the folder structure. ... The fuse graph operation in the APEX Autorig Component SOP, `merge_graph`, finds the new node, rig::ControlShape, and connects it into the original FK rig graph at the `spine_01` TransformObject node.\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nEach release has hundreds of enhancements. (https://media.sidefx.com/uploads/support/houdini_10yearsofinnovation_h21.pdf)\\nciteturn21search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2 months ago; KINEFX | Powered by APEX ... Python State Info Panels\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nCHARACTER FOUNDATIONS (https://media.sidefx.com/files/tutorials/kinefx-rigging-fur-dude/kinefx_rigging_fur_dude1.pdf)\\nciteturn21search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2 months ago; In this lesson, you will rig, animate and add fur to a two legged character named Fur Dude. ... This lesson uses KineFX, Houdini’s new SOP-based procedural rigging tools. ... © SideFX Software\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nCHARACTER FOUNDATIONS (https://media.sidefx.com/files/tutorials/foundations-rig-fur-dude-with-kinefx/hfoundations_07_kinefx_furdude.pdf)\\nciteturn21search14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3.2 years ago; To provide more flexibility with the control rig, you can add joints such as a root joint for the whole skeleton, heel joints for a ... Default State to: kinefx__rigpose.\\n\""}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"019f52de-5ebd-7b31-a4ae-90fc9573af35"}}}
