{"timestamp":"2026-07-11T20:29:53.248Z","ordinal":174,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call_output","call_id":"call_MRcPjoxD3E4aM4FfJhNjACSH","output":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Script completed\nWall time 1.8 seconds\nOutput:\n"},{"type":"input_text","text":"\"Unreal MCP in Unreal Editor | Unreal Engine 5.8 Documentation | Epic Developer Community (https://dev.epicgames.com/documentation/unreal-engine/unreal-mcp-in-unreal-editor)\\nciteturn30view0 [wordlim: 200] Content type: text/html; Source: open({\\\"ref_id\\\":\\\"turn28search1\\\",\\\"lineno\\\":null}); Total lines: 402\\nL11: Unreal MCP embeds an MCP server inside the Unreal Editor process so that any MCP-compatible AI agent, such as Claude Code, Cursor, or the MCP Inspector, can drive the editor over a local HTTP connection. The plugin exposes engine functionality, such as spawning actors, configuring lighting, creating material instances, inspecting Slate widgets, and running automation tests, as Tools that an AI agent can invoke on the user's behalf and is also easy to extend with your own custom tools.\\nL12: Keep in mind that many features are incomplete or missing. APIs and data formats are subject to change at any time as it matures.\\nL13: \\nL14: The plugin's identifier in the engine source tree, .uplugin files, C++ symbols, and console commands is ModelContextProtocol. The friendly name Unreal MCP is what surfaces in the Plugin Browser and in this documentation.\\nL15: \\nL16: Toolsets and tools are not implemented by Unreal MCP itself; instead, the AllToolsets plugin must be used to enable them.\\nL17: ##  What is the Model Context Protocol?\\nL18: \\nL19: The Model Context Protocol (MCP) is an open specification published at cite1†modelcontextprotocol.io†modelcontextprotocol.io . It defines how an AI client talks to an MCP server using a small set of JSON-RPC message types: initialize, tools/list, tools/call, and similar. A server advertises three kinds of primitives:\\nL20: \\nL21:   * Tools are named functions the client can call, with typed parameters and return values.\\nL22:   * Resources are read-only data the client can fetch by a Uniform Resource Identifier (URI).\\nL23: \\nL24:   * Prompts are reusable prompt templates.\\nL25: \\nL26: Unreal MCP is an MCP server: it advertises Tools backed by Unreal Engine functionality and accepts connections from any client that speaks the protocol.\\nL27: The plugin runs inside the editor process. By default, the server only accepts connections from the same machine, has no authentication layer, and is not designed for remote use. The exact binding behavior is documented under cite2†Limitations and Known Issues .\\nL28: \\nL29: A key function of the MCP server is to synchronize external requests with the Unreal Engine game thread by executing Tool invocations on the game thread serially, meaning clients should not issue overlapping Tool calls.\\nL30: ##  Setup\\nL31: \\nL32: cite34†Image L33: \\nL34: Setup follows these primary steps:\\nL35: \\nL36:   * Enable the Unreal MCP plugin.\\nL37: \\nL38:   * Configure auto-start.\\nL39: \\nL40:   * Generate a client configuration file.\\nL41: \\nL42:   * Start the AI agent from the project root.\\nL43: \\nL44:   * [Optional] Use the integrated Terminal plugin to run the AI agent, keeping the entire workflow inside the editor.\\nL45: ###  Enable the plugin\\nL46: \\nL47: Open Edit > Plugins, search for Unreal MCP, and check the Enabled box. The plugin depends on the Toolset Registry plugin, which is enabled automatically. Restart the editor when prompted.\\nL48: \\nL49: cite35†Image: Enable the Unreal MCP Plugin in the Plugins browser. Enable the Unreal MCP Plugin in the Plugins browser.\\nL50: ###  Configure auto-start\\nL51: \\nL52: Open Edit > Editor Preferences, scroll to the General group, and select Model Context Protocol. The panel exposes the Auto Start Server setting. When enabled, the MCP server starts automatically every time the editor launches and binds to `http://127.0.0.1:8000/mcp`.\\nL53: The same panel also exposes the listening port (default `8000`) and the URL path (default `/mcp`) for environments where those defaults conflict with another local service. The server name advertised in `serverInfo.name` is always `unreal-mcp`.\\nL54: \\nL55: cite36†Image: In Editor Preferences, enable Auto Start Server under Model Context Protocol. In Editor Preferences, enable Auto Start Server under Model Context Protocol.\\nL331: The server is therefore not strictly tied to the editor. Cooked and shipping game builds can host an MCP server by calling `IModelContextProtocolModule::StartServer()` at startup.\\nL358:   * The Toolset Registry adapter is editor-only. Cooked and shipping builds can host an MCP server (see Editor and runtime availability) but Tools shipped through the registry are not auto-discovered there; they must be registered explicitly through `IModelContextProtocolModule::AddTool()`.\\nL359: \\nL360:   * Live Coding does not propagate new `UFUNCTION` declarations. Adding a Tool requires an editor restart.\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nModelContextProtocol | Unreal Engine 5.8 Documentation | Epic Developer Community (https://dev.epicgames.com/documentation/unreal-engine/API/Plugins/ModelContextProtocol)\\nciteturn30view1 [wordlim: 200] Content type: text/html; Source: open({\\\"ref_id\\\":\\\"turn28search0\\\",\\\"lineno\\\":null}); Total lines: 276\\nL0: Table of Contents\\nL1: \\nL2:   1. cite73†Image: Epic Games†dev.epicgames.com cite0†Developer L3:   2. ModelContextProtocol\\nL4: \\nL5: # ModelContextProtocol\\nL6: \\nL7: On this page\\nL8: \\nL9: ## Navigation\\nL10: \\nL11: cite1†API > cite2†API/Plugins L12: \\nL13: Name ModelContextProtocol\\nL14: Type Plugin\\nL15: Part of Plugins cite3†Unreal MCP L16: Location /Engine/Plugins/Experimental/ModelContextProtocol/Source/ModelContextProtocol/\\nL17: Module Build Rules ModelContextProtocol.Build.cs\\nL37: cite19†FModelContextProtocolResourceDescriptorList List of Model Context Protocol resource descriptors that also tracks which cite17†IModelContextProtocolResourceProvider listed them\\nL38: cite20†FModelContextProtocolResourcesCapability L39: cite21†FModelContextProtocolRootsCapability L40: cite22†FModelContextProtocolSamplingCapability L41: cite23†FModelContextProtocolServerCapabilities L42: cite24†FModelContextProtocolServerInfo L43: cite25†FModelContextProtocolSession L44: cite26†FModelContextProtocolToolContext L45: cite27†FModelContextProtocolToolRequestId L46: cite28†FModelContextProtocolToolResult cite29†IModelContextProtocolTool execution result.\\nL47: cite30†FModelContextProtocolToolsCapability L48: cite17†IModelContextProtocolResourceProvider Abstract interface for exposing MCP (Anthropic's Model Context Protocol) resources.\\nL49: cite29†IModelContextProtocolTool Abstract interface for a describable MCP (Anthropic's Model Context Protocol) tool.\\nL50: ## Interfaces\\nL51: \\nL52: Name Remarks\\nL53: cite31†IModelContextProtocolModule L54: \\nL55: ## Enums\\nL56: \\nL57: ### Public\\nL58: \\nL59: Name Remarks\\nL60: cite32†EModelContextProtocolAudience L61: cite33†EModelContextProtocolSessionStatus L62: cite34†EModelContextProtocolToolResultType L63: cite35†UE::ModelContextProtocol::EToolNameValidation Tool name validation result per MCP spec (2025-11-25).\\nL64: ## Constants\\nL65: Name Type Remarks Include Path\\nL66: UE::ModelContextProtocol::ContentTypeEventStream const TCHAR *   ModelContextProtocol.h\\nL67: UE::ModelContextProtocol::DefaultServerName const TCHAR *   ModelContextProtocol.h\\nL68: UE::ModelContextProtocol::DefaultServerPort uint32   ModelContextProtocol.h\\nL69: UE::ModelContextProtocol::DefaultServerUrlPath const TCHAR *   ModelContextProtocol.h\\nL70: UE::ModelContextProtocol::JsonRpcVersion const TCHAR *   ModelContextProtocol.h\\nL71: UE::ModelContextProtocol::PODWrapperResultPropertyName const TCHAR *   ModelContextProtocol.h\\nL72: UE::ModelContextProtocol::ProtocolVersion const TCHAR * The latest protocol version supported by this server. ModelContextProtocol.h\\nL73: ## Functions\\nL74: ### Public\\nL75: \\nL76: Name Remarks Include Path cite36†Unreal Specifiers L77: \\nL78: bool cite37†operator! ( cite34†EModelContextProtocolToolResultType E\\nL79: )\\nL80: \\nL81:   ModelContextProtocolToolResults.h\\nL82: \\nL83: bool cite37†operator! ( cite32†EModelContextProtocolAudience E\\nL84: )\\nL85: \\nL86:   ModelContextProtocolToolResults.h\\nL87: \\nL88: cite34†EModelContextProtocolToolResultType cite38†operator& ( cite34†EModelContextProtocolToolResultType Lhs,\\nL89: cite34†EModelContextProtocolToolResultType Rhs\\nL90: )\\nL91: \\nL92:   ModelContextProtocolToolResults.h\\nL216: cite28†FModelContextProtocolToolResult cite52†UE::ModelContextProtocol::MakeStructuredContentResult ( const cite55†UObject * Object,\\nL217: const cite5†FJsonExportParams & Params\\nL218: )\\nL219: \\nL220:   ModelContextProtocolToolResults.h\\nL221: \\nL222: cite28†FModelContextProtocolToolResult cite52†UE::ModelContextProtocol::MakeStructuredContentResult ( const cite56†UStruct * StructDefinition,\\nL223: const void* Struct,\\nL224: const cite5†FJsonExportParams & Params\\nL225: )\\nL226: \\nL227:   ModelContextProtocolToolResults.h\\nL228: cite28†FModelContextProtocolToolResult cite52†UE::ModelContextProtocol::MakeStructuredContentResult ( cite57†FProperty * Property,\\nL229: const void* Value,\\nL230: const cite5†FJsonExportParams & Params\\nL231: )\\nL232: \\nL233:   ModelContextProtocolToolResults.h\\nL234: \\nL235: cite53†TSharedPtr < cite58†FJsonObject > cite59†UE::ModelContextProtocol::MakeTextContentObject ( TextT Text\\nL236: )\\nL237: \\nL238:   ModelContextProtocolToolResults.h\\nL239: \\nL240: cite28†FModelContextProtocolToolResult UE::ModelContextProtocol::MakeTextResult ( TextT Text\\nL241: )\\nL242:   ModelContextProtocolToolResults.h\\nL243: \\nL244: cite46†FString UE::ModelContextProtocol::NegotiateProtocolVersion ( const cite46†FString & ClientRequestedVersion\\nL245: )\\nL246: \\nL247: Returns the negotiated protocol version: the client's version if supported, otherwise the server's latest. ModelContextProtocol.h\\nL248: \\nL249: bool cite60†UE::ModelContextProtocol::SafeConvertRelativePathToFull ( const cite46†FString & BasePath,\\nL250: const cite46†FString & FileName,\\nL251: cite46†FString & OutFilePath\\nL252: )\\nL253: Joins BasePath & Filename, ensuring the resulting path is indeed under BasePath. ModelContextProtocolUtils.h\\nL254: \\nL255: cite35†EToolNameValidation cite61†UE::ModelContextProtocol::ValidateToolName ( const cite46†FString & ToolName\\nL256: )\\nL257: \\nL258: Validates a tool name per MCP spec (2025-11-25) tool name rules. ModelContextProtocol.h\\nL259: \\nL260: * * *\\nL261: \\nL262: Ask questions and help your peers cite62†Developer Forums†forums.unrealengine.com L263: \\nL264: Write your own tutorials or read those from others cite63†Learning Library L265: \\nL266: On this page\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nKineFX (https://www.sidefx.com/docs/houdini/character/kinefx/index.html)\\nciteturn30view2 [wordlim: 200] Content type: text/html; Source: open({\\\"ref_id\\\":\\\"turn21search2\\\",\\\"lineno\\\":null}); Total lines: 207\\nL0: cite0†  L1: \\nL2: cite0† Houdini 21.0  cite1† Character  L3: \\nL4: #  KineFX\\nL5: \\nL6: Procedural rigging and animation with SOP-based characters and rigs.\\nL7: \\nL8: cite84†Image L9: \\nL10: #### What is KineFX?\\nL11: \\nL12: KineFX is a rigging and animation framework and toolset that allows you to create and edit characters all at the geometry level. You can create your own KineFX characters from scratch, or use special KineFX SOPs and regular Houdini SOPs to edit imported characters and animation.\\nL13: \\nL14:   * For definitions of KineFX terminology, see the cite2†Glossary .\\nL15: #### Procedural rigging\\nL16: \\nL17: The KineFX framework is built on the principles of procedural rigging. Procedural rigging allows you to iterate on your rigs quickly, experiment non-destructively, and explore set-ups without fear of breaking anything like you would a static graph system. With KineFX, you can reconfigure a rig while animating, delete entire parts of an animated hierarchy and not lose its skin or animation, or unparent all your points and retain their positions (world space transforms).\\nL18: APEX (All-Purpose EXecution) is the graph evaluation framework that is used for building and evaluating character rigs. APEX graphs are geometry that are used to represent a character’s rig logic. Users can procedurally build and assemble rig logic pieces, and choose to evaluate the rig logic at a later point in the network. This idea of delayed evaluation allows the rig logic and rig evaluation to be decoupled, resulting in significant performance improvements.\\nL19: KineFX is the SOP-level rigging and animation framework, and APEX is the graph evaluation engine that runs underneath it. KineFX makes use of APEX, with some of the KineFX infrastructure now using APEX graph evaluation.\\nL20: \\nL21: cite85†Image L22: #### Animation and the packed character folder structure\\nL23: \\nL24: cite86†Image Packed character folder structure in the rig tree view\\nL25: To support the APEX animation and rigging workflows, the packed character folder structure is a way to define, bundle, and organize the data that is needed to create and animate one or more characters in the viewport. The packed character folder structure consists of geometry data that is organized similar to a directory structure on disk, with the data added as nested packed primitives.\\nL26: In the packed character folder structure, the elements of a character (skeleton, shape, rig) are added to a character container to build up a character, and characters are added to a scene container to assemble an animation scene. This animation scene is then passed to an APEX Scene Animate SOP, where animation can be performed in the node’s animate state.\\nL27: #### Animation retargeting\\nL28: \\nL29: cite87†Image Character model by Christophe Desse\\nL30: \\nL31: Retargeting is the process of taking animation from one character and transferring it to another character. KineFX’s flexibility and robustness allows it to handle differences in morphology and hierarchical structure between the source and target characters.\\nL32: \\nL33: ##  Subtopics cite3†¶ L34: \\nL35: ##  Overview cite4†¶ L36: \\nL37:   * cite5†KineFX character toolset L38: \\nL39:   * cite6†Maya transition guide L40: ##  Preparing character elements cite7†¶ L41: \\nL42:   * cite8†Working with skeletons L43: \\nL44:   * cite9†Preparing skeletons for rigging L45: \\nL46:   * cite10†Skeleton metadata L47: \\nL48:   * cite11†Creating capture weights on geometry L49: \\nL50:   * cite12†Character folder structure L51: ##  Rigging with APEX graphs cite13†¶ L52: \\nL53:   * cite14†APEX graph basics L54: \\nL55:   * cite15†APEX Script basics for graph building L56: \\nL57:   * cite16†Graphs for numerical operations L58: \\nL59:   * cite17†Graphs for viewport animation L60: \\nL61:   * cite18†Graphs for deforming geometry L62: \\nL63:   * cite19†Graph metadata L64: \\nL65:   * cite20†APEX path pattern L66: \\nL67:   * cite21†APEX graph nodes L68: \\nL69: APEX nodes provide operations for building up the functionality of APEX graphs, which are used in KineFX to create character rigs and perform other geometry manipulation.\\nL71: ##  Building rig graphs with APEX Script cite23†¶ L72: \\nL73:   * cite24†Working with graphs L74: \\nL75:   * cite25†Functions in APEX Script L76: \\nL77:   * cite26†Creating graph interfaces L78: \\nL79:   * cite27†Language reference L80: \\nL81: ##  Rigging with rig components cite28†¶ L82: \\nL83:   * cite29†Rigging with the Autorig Builder L84: \\nL85:   * cite30†Rigging a simple geometry L86: \\nL87:   * cite31†Rigging a character L88: \\nL89:   * cite32†Working with blend shapes L90: \\nL94: ##  Animating in the viewport cite35†¶ L95: \\nL96:   * cite36†Animation workflow L97: \\nL98:   * cite37†Working in the animate state L99: \\nL100:   * cite38†Posing L101: \\nL102:   * cite39†Motion paths L103: \\nL104:   * cite40†Constraints L105: \\nL106:   * cite41†Dynamic motion L107: \\nL108:   * cite42†Ragdoll L109: \\nL110:   * cite43†Full body IK L111: \\nL112:   * cite44†Animate state menus and HUDs L113: \\nL114:   * cite45†Animate state hotkeys L115: \\nL116:   * cite46†Control selection sets L117: \\nL118: A tree view of controls, selections, and selection sets for the animate state. Allows management of control selection and visibility.\\nL119: \\nL120:   * cite47†Motion mixer --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nPublic API (https://www.sidefx.com/docs/houdini/unreal/publicapi.html)\\nciteturn30view3 [wordlim: 200] Content type: text/html; Source: open({\\\"ref_id\\\":\\\"turn26search0\\\",\\\"lineno\\\":null}); Total lines: 993\\nL541: ##  Blueprint Async Processor cite12†¶ L542: \\nL543: The instantiation and cooking of the HDAs happen asynchonously in the plugin. Delegates help notify when HDA processing enters a certain phase or when cooking is complete. This means you have to manually bind and unbind delegates while writing scripts or tools using the API. To simplify this in Blueprints, the API also includes a Blueprint Async Action node: `UHoudiniPublicAPIProcessHDANode` or `ProcessHDA`.\\nL674: ##  Synchronous Processing cite14†¶ L675: \\nL676: In some case, using the public API asynchronously is not possible.\\nL677: \\nL678: This is the case for example, when using the api in a commandlet, as the commandlet will not tick the plugin’s asynchronous processor, and return immediately.\\nL679: \\nL680: To solve this issue, you can manually call the AssetWrapper’s ProcessComponentSynchronous  function (process_component_synchronous in python).\\nL681: This function is blocking, and will cook the wrapped Houdini Asset Component until it reaches an inactive state, fully instantiating, cooking, and processing it in one call.\\nL682: \\nL683: All registered delegates call (like OnPostInstantiate/Cook/Process) still happen when using this function, and can be used for additional operations on the HDA’s data.\\nL684: ##  PDG/TOP Networks cite15†¶ L685: \\nL686: The Public API supports interacting with the PDG asset link if an HDA contains one or more TOP networks.\\nL687: \\nL688: There are two delegates for PDG related events:\\nL689: \\nL690: Command\\nL691: \\nL692: Function\\nL693: \\nL694: `GetOnPostPDGTOPNetworkCookDelegate()`\\nL695: \\nL696: Broadcast after cooking a TOP Network in the HDA. Work item results do not have to be loaded yet\\nL697: \\nL698: `GetOnPostPDGBakeDelegate()`\\nL699: \\nL700: Broadcast after baking PDG outputs\\nL701: The following functions are available in UHoudiniPublicAPIAssetWrapper to interact with the PDG asset link:\\nL702: \\nL703: Command\\nL704: \\nL705: Function\\nL706: \\nL707: `HasPDGAssetLink()`\\nL708: \\nL709: Returns true if the wrapped asset is valid and has a PDG asset link\\nL710: \\nL711: `GetPDGTOPNetworkPaths()`\\nL712: \\nL713: Gets the paths (relative to the instantiated asset) of all TOP networks in the HDA\\nL714: \\nL715: `GetPDGTOPNodePaths()`\\nL716: \\nL717: Gets the paths (relative to the specified TOP network) of all TOP nodes in the network\\nL718: \\nL719: `PDGDirtyAllNetworks()`\\nL720: Dirty all TOP networks in this asset\\nL721: \\nL722: `PDGDirtyNetwork()`\\nL723: \\nL724: Dirty the specified TOP network\\nL725: \\nL726: `PDGDirtyNode()`\\nL727: \\nL728: Dirty the specified TOP node\\nL729: \\nL730: `PDGCookOutputsForNetwork()`\\nL731: \\nL732: Cook all outputs for the specified TOP network\\nL733: \\nL734: `PDGCookNode()`\\nL735: \\nL736: Cook the specified TOP node\\nL737: \\nL738: `PDGBakeAllOutputs()`\\nL739: \\nL740: Bake all outputs of the instantiated asset’s PDG contexts using the settings configured on the asset\\nL741: \\nL742: `PDGBakeAllOutputsWithSettings()`\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nAutomation Test Framework in Unreal Engine | Unreal Engine 5.8 Documentation | Epic Developer Community (https://dev.epicgames.com/documentation/en-us/unreal-engine/automation-test-framework-in-unreal-engine)\\nciteturn30view4 [wordlim: 200] Content type: text/html; Source: open({\\\"ref_id\\\":\\\"turn18search2\\\",\\\"lineno\\\":null}); Total lines: 95\\nL4: # Automation Test Framework\\nL5: \\nL6: Overview of the automation system used for unit testing, feature testing, and content stress testing.\\nL7: \\nL8: cite22†Image: Automation Test Framework L9: \\nL10: On this page\\nL11: The Automation System is built on top of the Functional Testing Framework, which is designed to do gameplay level testing, which works by performing one or more automated tests. Most tests that are written will be functional tests, low-level core or Editor tests that need to be written for using the Automation Framework system. These tests that are written can be broken down into the following categories depending on their purpose or function:\\nL12: Test Type Description\\nL13: Unit API level verification tests. See `TimespanTest.cpp` or `DateTimeTest.cpp` for examples of these.\\nL14: Feature System-level tests that verify such things as PIE, in-game stats, and changing resolution. See `EditorAutomationTests.cpp` or `EngineAutomationTests.cpp` for examples of these.\\nL15: Smoke Smoke tests are just considered a speed promise by the implementer. They are intended to be fast so they can run every time the Editor, game, or commandlet starts. They are also selected by default in the cite1†UI .  All Smoke tests are intended to complete within 1 second. Only mark Unit Tests or fast Feature Tests as Smoke Tests.\\nL16: Content Stress More thorough testing of a particular system to avoid crashes, such as loading all maps or loading and compiling all Blueprints. See `EditorAutomationTests.cpp` or `EngineAutomationTests.cpp` for examples of these.\\nL17: Screenshot Comparison This enables your QA testing to quickly compare screenshots to identify potential rendering issues between versions or builds.\\nL18: ## Overview\\nL19: \\nL20: You can use the Automation Test Framework to perform automated unit, feature, and content stress tests on your project.\\nL21: \\nL22: The Automation Test Framework is:\\nL23: \\nL24:   * Built directly in C++.\\nL25:   * Not associated with the `UObject` environment.\\nL26:   * Designed to function directly in Unreal Engine's core modules.\\nL27:   * Relies on Unreal Engine systems to work properly, so it is not ideal for pure unit testing. See cite2†Low-Level Tests for more information on pure unit testing.\\nL28: ## Interfaces\\nL29: \\nL30: Several interfaces are built on the framework to make writing tests easier, depending on your goals.\\nL31:   * cite3†Automation Spec : Supports Behavior Driven Design (BDD) methodology.\\nL32:   * cite4†Automation Driver : Supports user input simulation.\\nL33:   * cite5†Functional Testing : Supports Level testing with Blueprint.\\nL34:   * cite6†Screenshot Comparison Tool : Supports screenshot capture and comparison.\\nL35:   * cite7†FBX Test Builder : Supports FBX file testing.\\nL36:   * cite8†Editor Testing with Blueprint : Create Editor tests with Blueprints.\\nL37:   * cite9†Editor Testing with Python : Create Editor tests with Python.\\nL38:   * cite10†CQTest†github.com : Supports test syntax simplification for asynchronous execution and provides test fixtures.\\nL39: ## Tests in Plugins\\nL40: \\nL41: Containing tests in plugins has the following benefits. You can:\\nL42: \\nL43:   * Enable them individually.\\nL44:   * Choose to include them with the project packaged builds when compiled.\\nL45:   * Store content outside of the project's Content folder.\\nL46: ### Enabling Automation Test Plugins\\nL47: \\nL48: To enable the automation test plugins, do the following:\\nL49: \\nL50:   1. Select Edit > Plugins to open the Plugin panel.\\nL51:   2. In the Plugin panel, select the Testing filter on the left.\\nL52:   3. Enable the desired test plugins.\\nL53:   4. Restart the Unreal Editor.\\nL54: ## Test Design Guidelines\\nL55: \\nL56: Epic Games follows the following guidelines for our automation testing:\\nL57: \\nL58:   * Do not assume the state of the game or the Editor. Tests can run out of order or parallel across machines.\\nL59:   * Leave the state of the files on disk the way you found them. If a test generates a file, delete it when the test completes.\\nL60:   * Assume the test was left in a bad state the last time it ran. A good habit is generating and deleting files before the test starts.\\nL61: ## Running Automation Tests\\nL62: \\nL63:   1. Open any project.\\nL64:   2. Enable the cite11†Plugins available to use for testing and restart the Editor.\\nL65: \\nL66:   3. In the Editor, go to Window > Test Automation.\\nL67: \\nL68: For this menu option to be visible, you must first enable at least one cite11†automation tests plugin .\\nL69: \\nL70:   4. In the Automation tab of the Sessions Frontend under the Test Name column, enable the following:\\nL71:      * Editor\\nL72:      * Project\\nL73:      * System\\nL74:   5. In the Automation tab toolbar, click the Start Tests button. As the tests complete, you can follow the progress in the Test Name window.\\nL75: ## Essentials\\nL76: \\nL77: The Automation System provides the ability to perform Unit Testing, Feature Testing, and Content Stress Testing using the power of the Unreal Message Bus to increase stability.\\nL78: \\nL79:   * cite12† programming L80: \\nL81: * * *\\nL82: \\nL83: Ask questions and help your peers cite13†Developer Forums†forums.unrealengine.com L84: \\nL85: Write your own tutorials or read those from others cite14†Learning Library L86: \\nL87: On this page\\nL88:   * cite15†Overview L89:   * cite16†Interfaces L90:   * cite17†Tests in Plugins L91:   * cite11†Enabling Automation Test Plugins L92:   * cite18†Test Design Guidelines L93:   * cite19†Running Automation Tests L94:   * cite20†Essentials --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nIntroduction to PDG and TOPs (https://www.sidefx.com/docs/houdini/tops/intro.html)\\nciteturn30view5 [wordlim: 200] Content type: text/html; Source: open({\\\"ref_id\\\":\\\"turn15search10\\\",\\\"lineno\\\":null}); Total lines: 322\\nL3: #  Introduction to PDG and TOPs\\nL4: \\nL5: Explains the basic concepts behind TOP networks and what you can do with them.\\nL6: \\nL7:    On this page\\nL8: \\nL9:   * cite2†Overview L10: \\nL11:   * cite3†Traditional workflows vs. PDG workflows L12: \\nL13:   * cite4†Working with TOPs L14: \\nL15:     * cite5†TOP node UI L16: \\nL17:     * cite6†Processors L18: \\nL19:     * cite7†Partitions L20: \\nL21:     * cite8†Schedulers L22: \\nL23:   * cite9†Simplified TOP network example L24: \\nL25:   * cite10†Work items L26: \\nL27:     * cite11†Attributes L28: \\nL29:     * cite12†In-process vs. out-of-process L30: \\nL31:     * cite13†Static vs. dynamic L32: ##  Overview cite2†¶ L33: \\nL34: PDG stands for procedural dependency graph which is a procedural architecture that defines tasks and their dependencies to structure, schedule, and distribute work. You can use it to support automated and repeatable content pipelines in film, telvision, and real-time productions.\\nL35: PDG helps to solve limitations of more traditional and linear workflows. In these workflows, large tasks often run one after another even when some parts could run at the same time. PDG breaks the work into smaller, more manageable tasks and allows you to control how each task depends on the others. This makes it easier to use multiple CPU cores more efficiently and run tasks at the same time when possible. It also lets you update only the tasks that changed instead of redoing the entire workflow.\\nL36: Computing resources include local CPU cores, render farms, or cloud-based systems.\\nL37: TOPs stands for Task Operators, it helps to integrate PDG inside Houdini and provides dependency relationships through a node based network. A TOP network uses nodes that generate and organized work items, define dependencies, and pass data through attributes. TOPs is the network that helps orchestrate tasks that may involve geometry, simulation, rendering, machine learning, or external tools.\\nL38: ##  Traditional workflows vs. PDG workflows cite3†¶ L39: \\nL40: Although both workflows produce the same final output, PDG breaks the work into granular tasks and manages dependencies procedurally which allows for things to run in parallel and improves scalability.\\nL41: \\nL42: cite66†Image L43: \\nL44: ROP based workflow\\nL45: This example shows how a traditional ROP network handles a simple animation to render then a video pipeline versus the same pipeline in PDG. A ROP network processes work as a linear sequence. This means each stage must finish entirely before the next stage begins.\\nL46: \\nL47:   1. Generate the animation.\\nL48: \\nL49:      * The network generates the full animation sequence (ex. frame 1-10).\\nL50: \\nL51:      * This step must complete before rendering can start.\\nL52: \\nL53:   2. Render the animation.\\nL54:      * The renderer outputs each frame of the animation.\\nL55: \\nL56:      * Rendering does not begin until all animation output is ready.\\nL57: \\nL58:   3. Create the final video.\\nL59: \\nL60:      * Once every frame is rendered, a final tool such as FFmpeg ROP converts the image sequence into a video file.\\nL61: This results in downstream nodes not able to begin processing until all upstream nodes have full completed. This limits the scalability and doesn’t allow you to take full advantage of available parallelism on a local machine or render farm.\\nL62: \\nL63: PDG worfklow\\nL64: \\nL65: In PDG, the same workflow is broken down into individual tasks known as work items with dependencies between them. This allows the tasks to process in parallel and only wait on tasks that you set.\\nL66: \\nL67:   1. Animation task generation.\\nL68:      * PDG creates one work item per animation frame. For example, a 10 frame animation creates 10 “animation” tasks.\\nL69: \\nL70:      * Each task cooks independently.\\nL71: \\nL72:   2. Render task generation.\\nL73: \\nL74:      * For each animation frame, PDG generates a corresponding “Render frame” task.\\nL75: \\nL76:      * Each render tasks depends only on its matching animation. For example, render frame 6 waits only for animation frame 6.\\nL77: \\nL78:      * When a single animation frame is complete, its render task can begin immediately.\\nL79:   3. Video creation task.\\nL80: \\nL81:      * PDG creates a final “Create video” task.\\nL82: \\nL83:      * This task depends on all render tasks which ensures the full image sequence is available before stitching.\\nL84: \\nL85: The PDG workflow allows for individual tasks to begin executing as soon as their prerequisites have finished. You can also set depedency structures that define which tasks rely on which piece of data.\\nL86: ##  Working with TOPs cite4†¶ L87: \\nL88: cite67†Image L89: \\nL90: You can specify work to be done and how to manipulate the results of that work by building a network of cite14†TOP nodes . TOP nodes generate cite10†work items to do work and/or store information in cite11†attributes . There are several types of TOP nodes, but the two main types are: cite4†processors that generate new work items and cite4†partitions that create dependencies between items so they wait until all items in the partition finish.\\nL91: A TOP network describes a recipe for generating work items, efficiently running them in processes on a single machine or render farm, and figuring out dependencies so as many items can run in parallel as possible. This recipe is a PDG (Procedural Dependency Graph) that describes the work items to be completed and their dependencies.\\nL92: \\nL93: cite68†Image L94: \\nL95: cite69†Image \""}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"019f52de-5ebd-7b31-a4ae-90fc9573af35"}}}
